The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) has warned that climate change will have devastating effects on agriculture, fisheries, forestry, and other sectors of society. The IPCC has called for immediate action to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from agriculture and forestry, as well as to increase investment in sustainable development.
In 2015, the IPCC released its first assessment report, which concluded that “climate change poses a significant risk to human health and the environment”. It also noted that “the impacts of climate change are likely to be severe and irreversible.”
The IPCC has been working with governments around the world to address the issue of climate change through the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). These goals aim to:
- Reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 40% below 1990 levels by 2030;
- Ensure that all people everywhere have access to clean water and sanitation;
- Ensure that all people have access to safe drinking water;
- Ensure that all people have access to electricity and sanitation;
